<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<project source="4.1.0" version="1.0">
  A SYNTHETIC FIXTURE for the Swift port's FPGA bubble-tree gate. Hand-written, not corpus
  derived and not coursework: it exists to reach three paths of
  Netlist.constructHierarchyTree that no harvested circuit reaches.

  1. A subcircuit that CONTAINS bubbles, so a BUB SUB line carries a real global range instead
     of "-". Every harvested circuit puts its LEDs and buttons at the toplevel, so in that
     oracle every BUB SUB has all three counts zero and the subcircuit arithmetic is inert.
  2. The SAME subcircuit instantiated TWICE, which is the only way to reach
     enumerateGlobalBubbleTree at all: constructHierarchyTree recurses on the first instance of
     a circuit and takes the enumerate path on every later one.
  3. TWO levels of nesting, so enumerateGlobalBubbleTree's own recursion runs and the
     "startInputID + comp.getLocalBubbleInputStartId()" accumulation is exercised.

  On (3), the offset must be NON-ZERO or the term is invisible: `middle` therefore holds TWO
  leaf instances, so the second one's local start ids are 1 rather than 0. A first draft held
  one, and deleting the "+ getLocalBubbleInputStartId()" term from the port changed nothing
  because 0 + 0 == 0. That is the difference between a fixture that reaches a line and a fixture
  that can fail on it.

  Input bubbles (Button), output bubbles (LED) and a multi-bubble component (7-Segment Display,
  eight outputs) are mixed so the three counters advance independently rather than in lockstep.

  DELIBERATELY UNWIRED. Bubble enumeration walks mySubCircuits and myComponents and never looks
  at connectivity, and a subcircuit instance's port coordinates depend on its generated
  appearance - not something to hand-compute in a fixture. Unconnected pins are a DRC *warning*
  upstream, not an error, so the file passes DRC with nothing joined. An earlier draft did wire
  things up and failed DRC with a short circuit, because a Pin and a Button on one net are two
  sources.

  <lib desc="#Wiring" name="0"/>
  <lib desc="#I/O" name="5"/>
  <main name="top"/>
  <options/>
  <mappings/>
  <toolbar/>

  <circuit name="leaf">
    <a name="appearance" val="logisim_evolution"/>
    <a name="circuit" val="leaf"/>
    <comp lib="5" loc="(150,50)" name="Button">
      <a name="label" val="press"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="5" loc="(150,150)" name="LED">
      <a name="label" val="lamp"/>
    </comp>
  </circuit>

  <circuit name="middle">
    <a name="appearance" val="logisim_evolution"/>
    <a name="circuit" val="middle"/>
    <comp lib="5" loc="(50,150)" name="LED">
      <a name="label" val="midlamp"/>
    </comp>
    <comp loc="(250,50)" name="leaf">
      <a name="label" val="inner"/>
    </comp>
    <comp loc="(250,150)" name="leaf">
      <a name="label" val="other"/>
    </comp>
  </circuit>

  <circuit name="top">
    <a name="appearance" val="logisim_evolution"/>
    <a name="circuit" val="top"/>
    <comp lib="5" loc="(50,350)" name="7-Segment Display">
      <a name="label" val="digit"/>
    </comp>
    <comp loc="(300,50)" name="middle">
      <a name="label" val="first"/>
    </comp>
    <comp loc="(300,150)" name="middle">
      <a name="label" val="second"/>
    </comp>
    <comp loc="(300,250)" name="leaf">
      <a name="label" val="bare"/>
    </comp>
  </circuit>
</project>
